            0.124,
            0.775,
            0.159
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Cut a transverse and a longitudinal section of the ovary (swollen base of the pistil) and observe it under a dissecting microscope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.159,
            0.52,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Record any other feature(s) in Table 11.1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.175,
            0.767,
            0.194
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Draw a diagram of the structure you observed under the microscope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.196,
            0.775,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A complete flower has four parts — sepals, petals, stamens and pistil. You might have noticed that in some flowers both sepals and petals are fused. Can you guess the function of the most attractive part of a flower — the coloured petals? The stamen is the male part (Fig. 11.10). It consists of a filament and an anther, which produces pollen grains containing male gametes. The pistil is the female part that has three subparts — stigma, style and ovary (Fig. 11.11). The stigma is located at the tip, and may be flat and/ or sticky. The style is a thin long tube which connects the stigma to the ovary. The ovary contains ovules and each ovule has an egg cell (female gamete). You have learnt that the transfer of pollen from stamen to stigma is essential for the formation of fruits. How can you investigate it?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
